  :adr/body
  "## Context

`cloud-itonami-app` was started and signed into on this machine, and the Bots
surface (landed the same week) was exercised for the first time by its owner.
Two defects surfaced in the first minute of real use. Both were found by using
the thing, not by reading it — and both had already been *described correctly*
in a comment or a sibling screen that the failing code did not consult.

Sign-in itself was not broken. The server answered `/api/passkeys/authenticate/
start` correctly the whole time; what made it look broken was that Chrome's
window was 580x446, pinned by **yabai** on a Space that was not visible, so
nothing appeared to happen. Recorded because the README already warns about
exactly this (`open -n` flag coalescing, restored off-screen positions) and the
warning was still not enough: **AppleScript `set bounds` silently reverts under
a tiling window manager**, which the README does not say.

## Defect 1 — the picker filtered on the fact it had, not the fact that decides

'What do you use every day?' computed
`usable = enabled-tool-count > 0 && configurable?`.

GitHub carries two enabled tools and **no OAuth client on this machine**. So it
was offered, the first Bot was created holding its tools, and the only thing
that Bot could ever do was ask to connect — behind a button whose sole outcome
is `OAuth クライアントが未設定です`. Microsoft 365 was the same, unhit.

The grid's own comment states the intent it failed: *'offering it would be an
invitation to authorize an account for nothing'*. And **Settings had been right
all along** — it reads `provider['configured?']`, disables its button and says
`OAuth クライアント設定が必要です`. The fact was computed and published; the
Bots surfaces were not reading it.

`bots/provider-authable?` is now that one fact, carried by `catalog` and by the
connection card.

**The two reasons are reported apart.** 'no enabled tool in this build' and 'no
OAuth client on this machine' are fixed on different screens, so collapsing
them into one disabled tile sends a person to the wrong one. This is the line
ADR-2608095000 draws between `:server-side` and `:not-found`, and the line
`route-scan` draws between `missing` and `unresolvable`.

`:card/authable?` is a FIELD, not a fifth `connection-states` member: an
offered card with no client is still offered, and would be offered tomorrow if
somebody configured one.

### The half-fix, and what it taught

The first commit reached only cards written after it. **Cards are persisted
inside messages**, so the Bot already on the machine kept rendering 認証する.
Whether this machine can authorize a provider is not something the conversation
said once — it is the state of the installation at the moment somebody looks at
the button, and it moves in both directions. `public-card` therefore recomputes
it on read; the stored value stays as the record of what was true when offered.

Found only because the fix was deployed to the running instance and looked at.
A test-green change that does not reach the screen the owner is on is not done.

## Defect 2 — 96px of padding outside the box

The document scrolled sideways, clipping the create form's 名前 field and
はじめる button off the right edge. Measured in the running app rather than
guessed:

| element | overflow | cause |
|---|---|---|
| `.bots-onboard` | **96px** | `width:100%` + `padding:3rem` under default `content-box` |
| `.bots-thread__scroll` | 32px | same (`padding:1rem`) |
| `.bots-card` | 30px | same (`padding:.875rem`) |

The overflow equalled the padding exactly, and propagated up `.bots-shell` →
`main` → `.workspace` → `body`.

This stylesheet has **no global `border-box`**; every padded rule declares it
individually (`.view`, `.sidebar`, `.composer`, `.chat-thread`, …). Three
sibling rules written together all omitted it — which is what a per-rule
convention costs. Stated once for the view instead of patched three times:
every box here stretches to a fixed grid column and has padding, so
`border-box` is the only sizing that can be right, and a fourth such box will
be written.

## Decision

1. A Bots surface must not offer an authorization this installation cannot
   perform, and must name WHICH of the two reasons applies.
2. Installation state (`authable?`) is answered on read, never replayed from a
   stored message.
3. A view laid out in a fixed grid column declares `box-sizing` for the view,
   not per rule.

## Verification

- Full JVM suite after each change: **1353 → 1354 → 1355 tests**, 7669 → 7671 →
  7672 assertions, **0 failures / 0 errors** throughout.
- The connector tests were confirmed to **FAIL (2 failures) against a
  deliberately wrong `provider-authable?`** before landing — the gate was seen
  to fail before it was trusted.
- `node --check` on `interaction.js`.
- Re-measured in the running instance after deploy: `sidewaysScrollbar: false`,
  every box `border-box` and equal to its parent's client width; the existing
  Bot's card renders a disabled 未設定 with
  `この端末に OAuth クライアントがありません`; the picker greys GitHub and
  Microsoft 365 with `OAuth クライアント設定が必要です` while Google
  Calendar / Drive / Gmail stay selectable.

## Landed

`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app` default branch: `ee232f5`, `167a6b9`,
`d178827`. The west pin was already at `0cb37d2` (a later tip), so all three
are contained and no pin advance was required.

## Not done, and why

- **GitHub and Microsoft 365 remain unusable** until an OAuth client exists.
  GitHub has no API for creating an OAuth App (web UI only), and the secret is
  a credential — both outside the standing authorization's safety floor. The
  owner creates the app (callback
  `http://localhost:1338/api/oauth/github/callback`) and stores it themselves
  with `security add-generic-password -U -s gftd.github -a CLIENT_ID -w`
  (value-less `-w` prompts hidden, keeping the secret out of shell history and
  process arguments). No restart is needed: `configured-value` reads the
  keychain per call.
- **Google is configured and unconnected** (`google-oauth-client` /
  `personal-warehouse` present). One consent away from 12 live tools, flagged
  to the owner because the grant includes `gmail.modify`.
- The `.kotoba` decision core was not touched; `authable?` is host knowledge
  (an installation fact), not an admission input, so `bot_core.kotoba`'s four
  booleans are unchanged."}]
